  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe central idea is to optimize the step function h using a customized mixed-strategy optimization algorithm that leverages both local and global search methods. This approach combines the speed of gradient-based methods with the robustness of population-based optimization to escape local minima. Unlike the original program, which relies on a single optimizer, the proposed method will:\n\n- Use a differential evolution algorithm for global exploration\n- Combine it with a projected gradient method for local refinement\n- Employ a multi-resolution strategy to search different feature scales\n- Use a carefully constructed surrogate objective function that approximates the true maximum correlation while maintaining mathematical properties\n\nThis multi-phase strategy addresses the challenges of:\n\n1) The non-convex nature of the optimization landscape which can trap gradient-based methods\n2) The need to maintain integral normalization and bounds (0,1) for each step\n3) The computational cost constraints that prevent exhaustive searches\n\nThe approach is distinguished by its adaptive hyperparameter scheduling based on elapsed time and its dimensionality-aware optimization to maintain efficiency across different resolutions. The method ensures mathematical consistency by:\n- Properly incorporating the original objective definition\n- Adhering to the given constraints\n- Maintaining convergence to a feasible solution while progressively improving the upper bound\n\nKey improvements over the previous method include:\n\n1) First-order gradient information is maintained during optimization to accelerate convergence\n2) The solution representation ensures mathematical consistency with the original problem setup\n3) A novel repair mechanism preserves the integral constraint while enforcing bounds\n4) The surrogate function balances between computational efficiency and fitness approximation quality\n\nHowever, this approach assumes that the optimal h may exhibit periodic patterns (helpful for correlation reduction) and may not guarantee finding the absolute global minimum, but provides a robust balance between quality and speed.\n\n2. Mathematical specifications\n\nVariables and representation:\n- Discrete representation: h \u2208 \u211d\u207f with values in [0,1] (each entry)\n- Constraint: \u2211_{i=1}^n h_i = n/2 (integral = 1 when scaled appropriately)\n- The correlation is computed as C = max_{k} \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx over all integer lags k\n\nObjective formulation:\nC_5(h) = maxx\u2208{0,1}\u207f [max_{k} \u2211_{i} \u03d5(i,i+k)h_i(1-h_{i+k})]\u0394x\nwhere \u0394x = 2/n\n\nFor optimization:\n1. We use a Surrogate Objective Function (SOF) that approximates the maximum correlation:\n   S(h) = min_{\u03b1>0} { \u03b1^(-1)log(\u2211_{k} exp(\u03b1 \u00b7 c_k)) }\nwhere c_k is the correlation at lag k\n\n2. Constraints handling:\n   - Integral constraint: solved with projection\n   - Pointwise bounds: enforced via clipping to [0,1]\n   - SOF gradients are computed via:\n   \u2207S(h) \u2248 \u03b1^{-1} [c_k - m] \u2211_{\u2113} exp(\u03b1\u00b7c_\u2113) ] \u00b7 gradient of correlator at lag k\n\nFormulations:\n- Projection onto feasible set: \n  P(h) = clip(h + (c - n/2)/n, 0,1)   where c = \u2211 h_i\n\n- Mixed-integer search:\n  Use population-based methods for continuous variables with genetic crossover for diversity\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nPhase initialization (seed=42, budget=1100s):\n- Start with parent solution from initial_h_values (n=500)\n- Convert to native representation for optimization\n\nAlgorithm definition:\ndef optimize_strategy(n_samples: int, initial=None, time_budget=None):\n\nHelper functions:\n\ndef project_feasible(h, n):\n    # h: np array of size n\n    # Clip and adjust sum\n    h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n    current_sum = np.sum(h)\n    diff = current_sum - n/2\n    h += diff / n\n    h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n    return h.clip(0, 1)\n\ndef compute_surrogate(h, n, alpha=1.0):\n    # Compute the smooth surrogate value\n    c_all = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * (2/n)\n    m = c_all.max()\n    if alpha == 0:\n        return 0\n    # Handle large alpha\n    try:\n        shift = alpha * (c_all - m)\n        log_term = np.log(np.sum(np.exp(shift)))\n        return log_term / alpha\n    except:\n        return m\n\ndef compute_surrogate_gradient(h, n, alpha=1.0):\n    # Gradient approximation\n    try:\n        c_all = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * (2/n)\n        m = c_all.max()\n        shift = alpha * (c_all - m)\n        log_term = np.log(np.sum(np.exp(shift)))\n        w = np.exp(shift) / np.sum(np.exp(shift-np.min(shift)))\n        grad_contrib = {}\n        for lag_idx, lag_val in enumerate([-len(h)+1, len(h)-1]):\n            grad = _compute_lag_gradient(h, n, lag_val, alpha)\n            grad_contrib[lag_idx] = w[lag_idx] * grad\n        return np.sum(list(grad_contrib.values()))\n    except:\n        return np.zeros(n)\n\nCore optimization:\n\n1. Global phase (max 50% budget):\n   - Use differential evolution on surrogate function\n   - Population size = sqrt(n)\n   - Dimension-aware mutation factor\n   - Early termination if time runs out\n\n2. Local phase (remaining budget):\n   - Use SGD with momentum on projected gradient\n   - Adaptive learning rate\n   - Momentum term of 0.95\n   - Connective gradient computations:\n     g = compute_surrogate_gradient(x, n)\n     x_new = project_feasible(x - lr*g, n)\n\n3. Multi-resolution integration:\n   - For each candidate h_{best} from global phase:\n     Check performance on smaller resolutions (n=200, 300)\n   - Use interpolation and projection for transfer between resolutions\n\n4. Time management:\n   - Budget allocation: 50% global, 50% local\n   - Monitor elapsed time at start of major steps\n   - Gradient computations include finite-difference approximation for stability\n\nConstraint handling:\n   - Optimization maintains continuous representation\n   - Feasible solutions enforced via projection method\n   - Infeasible solutions repaired using mean adjustment\n\nStopping criteria:\n   - No improvement of 1e-4 in surrogate value\n   - Time budget exceeded\n   - 1000 consecutive evaluations without improvement\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\nDefault configuration:\n   - Global phase: DE with crossover rate 0.9, mutation factor 0.7\n   - Local phase: Momentum SGD with base learning rate 0.05\n   - Resolution: start with parent resolution to avoid recomputation\n   - Surrogate temperature: \u03b1 schedules from 0.1 to 5.0\n\nTriggered variations:\n   - When time < 20%, switch to aggressive sampling:\n     - Mutation factor increases to 0.95\n     - Stop evaluation if any step exceeds 5% improvement threshold\n\n5. Budget estimation and robustness\n\nTime estimates for n=500:\n   - Projection operations: O(n) per evaluation = 500*2000s = 1e6 operations per method call\n   - Gradient computation: O(n\u00b2) per evaluation = 500\u00b2 * 1500s = 1.25e9 operations for local phase\n   - Global phase effectiveness: expects 10,000 evaluations\n\nFeasibility fallback:\n   - If global phase never finds solution, start from uniform random domain point\n   - If two resolutions give infeasible, use projected gradient on parent resolution only\n\nProgress monitoring:\n   - Time watch at start of global and local phases\n   - Quit global phase early if correlation value stabilizes\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nFinal candidate checks:\n   - Feasibility verification (integral = n/2 within 1e-5)\n   - All values within [0,1] (after projection)\n   - Complete evaluation of max correlation\n\nCommon pitfalls avoidance:\n   - Avoid division by zero in gradients (handle m equal to infinity)\n   - Clip gradient values to [-10,10] to prevent explosions\n   - Ensure surrogate approximates the true maximum correlation\n\nReturn format:\n   (h_final, final_c5, n)\n\nThe method maintains the mathematical integrity of the original problem while introducing a stable surrogate for the optimization process. The separation of concerns between surrogate estimation and constraint handling preserves the problem's boundary conditions.\n\nNote: Unlike the original code, this formulation does not attempt to minimize all lags simultaneously but focuses improvement by schedule-based hyperparameter adaptation. The method is designed for progressive improvement without assuming any functional form for the optimal h.",
